    {
      "path": "/workspace/chen-imbalanced-semiprimes/paper/release-20261106/en/sieve-appendix.tex",
      "sha256": "15c9f561b870edb43ec1de3a8adf88b0b4561982f0617b58b26b1de966f9f9d8",
      "lines": [
        778,
        793
      ],
      "text": "For a switched domain $\\mathcal D_i$ with $r$ primes, let\n$w=u_{r-1}$ and $U=u_1+\\cdots+u_r$.  Its normalized unsifted\nmass at the actual parent ratio $\\alpha$ is\n\\begin{equation}\\label{sv:full-switched-mass}\n \\mathcal M_i(\\alpha)=\n \\int_{\\substack{\\boldsymbol u\\in\\mathcal D_i\\\\U\\le\\alpha-w}}\n \\frac{\\omega((\\alpha-U)/w)}{w\\prod_j u_j}\\,d\\boldsymbol u\n +\\int_{\\substack{\\boldsymbol u\\in\\mathcal D_i\\\\U=\\alpha}}\n                     \\frac{du_1\\cdots du_{r-1}}{\\prod_j u_j}.\n\\end{equation}\nIn the surface integral one coordinate is eliminated with derivative\none.  The surface counts quotient $1$; it is separate from the\nordinary Buchstab term.  Prime summation and the rough-number\nformula on fixed positive boxes give \\eqref{sv:full-switched-mass}.\nThe upper count in parent units is $K\\mathcal M_i(\\alpha)/4$.\n",
      "scope": "Quotient-one surface remains separate from ordinary Buchstab term; this finite equality does not discard or asymptotically bound it."
    },
    {
      "path": "/workspace/chen-imbalanced-semiprimes/paper/release-20261106/en/argument.tex",
      "sha256": "87f5b5f2e25082e00b2b04d8ecc684630d64846912457dc0adade1a36fd67182",
      "lines": [
        531,
        536
      ],
      "text": "Map a contributing four-prefix tuple to its last pair $(p_3,p_4)$.\nThe quotient roughness forces its first two selected primes to be\nthe two least factors of $n$. The image lies in the prescribed\nrectangle, and removing $p_3p_4$ leaves an $N^{a_{\\rm s}}$-rough residual,\nso this pair contributes to $Y_6(\\mathscr E)$. Since the first two factors\nare determined by $n$, two tuples with the same last pair coincide.",
      "scope": "Existing ordered last pair and prescribed rectangle retain actual labels."
    },
    {
      "path": "/workspace/chen-imbalanced-semiprimes/paper/release-20261106/en/argument.tex",
      "sha256": "87f5b5f2e25082e00b2b04d8ecc684630d64846912457dc0adade1a36fd67182",
      "lines": [
        541,
        548
      ],
      "text": "$|W_{\\mathscr E}|$ by $1016$ on rough $n$.  The number of $n\\le N$ with a\nrepeated prime at least $N^{a_{\\rm s}}$ is at most $2N^{1-{a_{\\rm s}}}$ for large $N$.\nAfter restricting to primes $p<N$ with $(p,N)=1$ and removing the\nunit, the squareful and unit costs are at most\n$2032N^{1-{a_{\\rm s}}}+4=o(H_N)$.  It follows that\n\\begin{equation}\n 4D_{1,2}(N)\\ge\n       \\sum_{\\substack{p<N\\\\p\\ {\\rm prime}\\\\(p,N)=1}}W_{\\mathscr E}(N-p)",
      "scope": "The unit removed separately at weight-to-count is not inferred negligible by this exact carrier repair."
    }
